    - target: {fileID: 1589925493925568863, guid: 76354cb641cf5aa46ba4902d9b7024f7, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: Enschede was rebuilt after a massive fireworks disaster in 2000 changed
        the city forever.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 6012603428257306392, guid: 76354cb641cf5aa46ba4902d9b7024f7, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: The city sits so close to Germany that some buses casually cross the
        border mid-route.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 6380436179282662226, guid: 76354cb641cf5aa46ba4902d9b7024f7, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: "<font=\"bold\">Enschede</font> is the main railway station in Enschede,
        Netherlands. \nThe station opened on 1 July 1866 and is on the Zutphen\u2013Glanerbeek
        railway. Between the late 1970s and 2001, the passenger service to Germany
        stopped. The connection to M\xFCnster was reopened in 2001. There is no connection
        allowing the German trains to run any further into Overijssel; however there
        was before the line closed. "
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
